          "heading": "What Is Federalism? Key Features",
          "para": "Federalism is a system of government in which power is divided between a central authority and various constituent units of the country - usually called states or provinces. Unlike a unitary system, where the national government holds all powers and merely delegates some, a federation has two or more levels of government, each governing the same citizens but in different spheres. Its key features: first, two or more levels of government; second, each level has its own jurisdiction in legislation, taxation and administration, guaranteed by a written constitution that neither level can unilaterally change; third, the highest court - a supreme or federal court - interprets the constitution and settles disputes between levels; fourth, each level has its own sources of revenue so it can function; fifth, the federal system has dual objectives - to safeguard and promote unity of the country while accommodating regional diversity. Governments at both levels are elected by - and answerable to - the people, which makes federalism democratic, not merely administrative.",
