          "heading": "The Constitutional Division: Union, State and Concurrent Lists",
          "para": "The Indian constitution distributes legislative power through three lists. The Union List contains subjects of national importance - defence, foreign affairs, banking, currency, communications - originally about 97 subjects, on which only the Union Parliament can legislate. The State List contains subjects of state and local importance - police, trade and commerce within the state, agriculture, irrigation - on which state assemblies legislate. The Concurrent List holds subjects of common interest - education, forests, trade unions, marriage, adoption, succession - on which both levels may legislate, but a Union law overrides a conflicting state law. Subjects not in any list - residuary subjects - newly emerging areas such as cyber law - fall to the Union under residuary powers. Union Territories are administered by the central government. If any dispute arises over the division, the Supreme Court decides - the judiciary is the federation's referee.",
